      "lem:santanna-zhao-panel-did-gradient"
    ],
    "justification": "This reduction checks that the new envelope collapses to the established two-group panel-DiD bound when union geometry disappears.",
    "gap": "SantAnnaZhao2020 gives the two-group panel-DiD bound but does not derive it as the nested special case of a PT-or-SC union envelope.",
    "consumer": "The reduction provides a benchmark against the maintained two-period DiD routines already used by empirical researchers.",
    "proof_tex": "If \\(J=1\\), then \\(\\mathbf 1_J\\mathbf 1_J^{\\prime}=1\\), and \\(\\mathrm{def{:}restriction\\text{-}maps}\\) gives\n\\[\n A_{\\mathrm{PT}}=I_1-1=0,\n \\qquad\n r_{\\mathrm{PT},x}=0.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe tangent normal form therefore yields\n\\[\n \\mathcal T_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)=\\mathcal H_{P_0},\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)=\\{\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\}.\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nIn particular, the SC tangent space is a subspace of the unrestricted PT observed tangent space.\n\nDerivative compatibility must still be checked. With one donor, the intercept restriction forces \\(w_2(x)=1\\), and the SC restriction gives\n\\[\n \\mu_{1,t}(x)=\\mu_{2,t}(x),\n \\qquad t<T.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThus at every nearby law in the SC branch,\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\theta_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_O)\n &=\\pi_1^{-1}\\sum_xp_X(x)p_1(x)\n \\{\\mu_{1,T}(x)-\\mu_{1,T-1}(x)-\\mu_{2,T}(x)+\\mu_{2,T-1}(x)\\}\\\\\n &=\\pi_1^{-1}\\sum_xp_X(x)p_1(x)\n \\{\\mu_{1,T}(x)-\\mu_{2,T}(x)\\}\n =\\theta_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_O),\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{4}\n\\]\nwhere (3) at \\(t=T-1\\) gives the second equality. Hence the two functional derivatives agree along every SC tangent score. Since \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\) represents the derivative of \\(\\theta_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\) on all of \\(\\mathcal H_{P_0}\\), equation (4) implies \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\in\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_0)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n \\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)\\subseteq\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_0),\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)\\cap\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_0)\n =\\{\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\}.\n \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe definitions of \\(\\phi_U\\) and \\(V_U\\) now give \\(\\phi_U=\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\) and \\(V_U=V_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\).\n\nFor \\(T=2\\), write \\(\\Delta Y=Y_2-Y_1\\) and\n\\[\n m_g(x)=E_O[\\Delta Y\\mid G=g,X=x].\n\\]\nOverlap gives \\(p_1(x)>0\\), \\(p_2(x)>0\\), and \\(\\pi_1>0\\), so every following division is valid. Direct differentiation of the PT observed-law functional gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}(O)\n &=\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=1\\}}{\\pi_1}\\{\\Delta Y-m_1(X)\\}\n -\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=2\\}}{\\pi_1}\\frac{p_1(X)}{p_2(X)}\n   \\{\\Delta Y-m_2(X)\\}\\\\\n &\\quad+\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=1\\}}{\\pi_1}\n \\{m_1(X)-m_2(X)-\\theta\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe first two terms have conditional mean zero. The last is centered because \\(\\theta=E_O[m_1(X)-m_2(X)\\mid G=1]\\), and it is the derivative contribution from the treated covariate distribution. Thus (6) is mean zero, and direct pathwise differentiation verifies its covariance equation for every unrestricted observed score. Under the relabeling of treated and comparison indicators as \\(G=1\\) and \\(G=2\\), equation (6) is the panel ATT efficient influence function in \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}santanna\\text{-}zhao\\text{-}panel\\text{-}did\\text{-}gradient}\\).",
